  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Patchy dense freezing fog this morning with reduced visibility\n   and slick areas of roadway in Northwest Montana.\n\n-  Mild and showery conditions with overnight fog and low stratus\n   continue through Monday with generally light precipitation \n   amounts.\n\n-  Colder air arrives Tuesday and Wednesday, bringing mountain\n   snow and a mix of rain and snow to valleys.\n\nStrong thermal inversions are established across western Montana \nthis morning, with mid-slope terrain temperatures running up to 15\ndegrees warmer than the valley floors. However, mid and high-\nlevel cloud cover streaming in from the southwest has inhibited \nthe efficient radiational cooling and saturation necessary for \nprolonged and widespread dense fog formation south of the I-90 \ncorridor. Areas of dense fog and freezing fog remain present in \nthe Mission, Flathead, and lower Clark Fork valleys. Road \nsurfaces, especially bridges and overpasses, may become slick in \nthese areas. The likelihood of prolonged periods of widespread\novernight and morning fog will continue to linger, but eventually\ndecrease, heading through the weekend.\n\nA series of weak troughs embedded in southwesterly flow will \nbring widely scattered light showers to the region today through \nMonday. Precipitation amounts will remain light. The 25th to 75th \npercentile spread for precipitation totals through Monday evening\ngenerally ranges from a few hundredths up to 0.20 inches in the \nvalleys, with up to 0.50 inches possible in the central Idaho \nmountains. Snow levels will remain above 5,000 to 6,000 feet \nthrough the weekend. Light southwesterly winds will develop Sunday\nand Monday, with the strongest winds and gusts focused along \nmountain passes and higher terrain.\n\nA stronger thermal gradient and transient surface low will track\nthrough the region Tuesday into Wednesday, introducing a colder\nairmass. Snow levels will become highly diurnal, dropping to near\n3,000 feet, or lower, during overnight periods. Minor snow \naccumulations (up to a couple inches) are expected for the higher\nelevations of Central Idaho, Lemhi County, and Southwest Montana,\nincluding locations such as Lost Trail, Georgetown Lake, and \nHomestake Pass. Valley precipitation will generally fall as\npredominantly rain or a rain/snow mix during the day, possibly \ntransitioning to light snow overnight near and above that 3,000\nfoot mark. \n\nFor this Tuesday through Thursday system, the 25th to \n75th percentile spread for precipitation totals ranges from 0.10 \nto 0.50 inches in western Montana valleys, with heavier amounts \nacross central Idaho and southwest Montana. While valley floor \nsnow accumulations will be minimal, any overnight snowfall will \nmelt during the day with the potential to re-freeze into patchy \nice particularly on elevated roadways, bridges, and overpasses \nduring subsequent overnight periods. Another low pressure system \ndropping south into California by Thursday will maintain \nsouthwesterly flow and periodic light showers across the Northern \nRockies.",
